WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:05.390
Hey Thoughty2 here. The room is dark, the
duvet is wrapped warm around you, you’ve

00:00:05.390 --> 00:00:10.260
finally put your damn phone down and you wonder
“why am I not asleep yet?”

00:00:10.260 --> 00:00:15.780
Sometimes, no matter how much you want to,
you simply cannot find the off button. You’ve

00:00:15.780 --> 00:00:21.119
tried everything; counting sheep, breathing
exercises, listening to whale songs, you’ve

00:00:21.119 --> 00:00:26.019
even tried counting whales and listening to
sheep songs but nothing is working. Why does

00:00:26.019 --> 00:00:31.579
this happen? If mother nature decided that
we needed to spend a third of the day recharging,

00:00:31.579 --> 00:00:35.070
then why did she make it so bloody difficult
to do it.

00:00:35.070 --> 00:00:39.860
We all know how frustrating it can be so let’s
try to understand what’s preventing you

00:00:39.860 --> 00:00:47.330
from dosing off and how you can help it along
a little.

00:00:47.330 --> 00:00:53.810
First things first; what is the difference
between being awake and being asleep? You

00:00:53.810 --> 00:00:59.660
close your eyes, you lie still, you breathe
deeply, you can even try dribbling a little,

00:00:59.660 --> 00:01:04.750
but something special has to happen for you
to make the mysterious switch to the other

00:01:04.750 --> 00:01:08.380
side.
There are 5 stages of sleep. In the first,

00:01:08.380 --> 00:01:13.460
you move from an Alpha state, basically daydreaming,
into a Theta state, where your brain locks

00:01:13.460 --> 00:01:18.219
down your muscles and prepares to move into
deeper sleep. You could say that you’re

00:01:18.219 --> 00:01:24.189
technically asleep in Theta, but very lightly.
It’s at this stage where the Hypnic Jerk

00:01:24.189 --> 00:01:29.079
can occur, that moment when you suddenly twitch
awake as if you’re falling. We think it’s

00:01:29.079 --> 00:01:34.700
likely to be caused by your brain still being
conscious when sleep paralysis sets in. It

00:01:34.700 --> 00:01:39.280
could have been an evolutionary benefit so
that our old primate ancestors didn’t fall

00:01:39.280 --> 00:01:45.079
out the tree when they had a little nap on
a branch. But, given that, in the US alone,

00:01:45.079 --> 00:01:52.201
1.8 million people visit the emergency room
every year because they fell out of bed, evolution

00:01:52.201 --> 00:01:56.939
has quite a long way to go.
In stage two, your brain sends out waves called

00:01:56.939 --> 00:02:02.399
sleep spindles that we think are used as a
sort of thought-jammer to keep you calm and

00:02:02.399 --> 00:02:08.410
stop you thinking about the washing up or
that spreadsheet or where you hid the bodies.

00:02:08.410 --> 00:02:13.280
Stage 3 is the transition from light sleep
to deep sleep and stage 4 is when you get

00:02:13.280 --> 00:02:19.190
a rush of delta waves and you are deepest
asleep. Stage 4 last about 30 minutes and

00:02:19.190 --> 00:02:24.200
is the best time to draw on someone’s face.
Finally, you get stage 5, when REM occurs

00:02:24.200 --> 00:02:29.280
and this is when your body repairs itself,
jots down memories permanently and makes you

00:02:29.280 --> 00:02:33.690
feel rested.
But the big question for today is; what kicks

00:02:33.690 --> 00:02:39.890
off this whole process? What happens to lead
you to the Theta state in stage one, where

00:02:39.890 --> 00:02:44.250
it all begins?
So there are two main factors that control

00:02:44.250 --> 00:02:50.070
sleepiness. One is sleep pressure, something
that increases the longer you’ve been awake,

00:02:50.070 --> 00:02:56.130
and is created by the release of. The level
rises while you’re awake and then you break

00:02:56.130 --> 00:03:02.700
it down when you sleep. The next factor is
the light. We may now have 24hr supermarkets

00:03:02.700 --> 00:03:08.230
and skype calls at 3am but we’re not designed
for this. We’re supposed to get up at sunrise

00:03:08.230 --> 00:03:13.950
and sleep at sunset, like the Tellytubbies.
This is done through melatonin which is released

00:03:13.950 --> 00:03:19.330
when it’s dark, helping you to feel sleepy.
As the sun rises, your body releases cortisol

00:03:19.330 --> 00:03:24.340
which gets you up and ready for another day
in your repetitive and pointless existence.

00:03:24.340 --> 00:03:31.670
Okay, so what happens to interrupt this complicated
process? One day you’re snoring like a pig

00:03:31.670 --> 00:03:36.460
in a blanket and then bam! you’re up at
4am watching youtube for the fifth night in

00:03:36.460 --> 00:03:42.430
a row and, although this video is both entertaining
and educational, you’re welcome by the way,

00:03:42.430 --> 00:03:48.040
you’d probably rather be dreaming of chocolate
dinosaur land and riding a Tyrannosaurus Twix,

00:03:48.040 --> 00:03:51.400
and not waking up feeling like you’ve been
in a cage fight.

00:03:51.400 --> 00:03:56.530
Stress is probably the main cause of “why
can’t I f’in sleep”, because stress

00:03:56.530 --> 00:04:01.580
just throws all of your systems out of whack;
hormone levels go all over the place, heart

00:04:01.580 --> 00:04:07.150
rhythms change, it’s a mess. So you need
to manage this through tackling the cause

00:04:07.150 --> 00:04:11.730
of your stress. Not saying that’s easy,
but there’s plenty of professional support

00:04:11.730 --> 00:04:16.190
out there. Sorry I can’t help you on that
front.

00:04:16.190 --> 00:04:21.720
Then there’s drugs; both illegal and legal.
Caffeine, for example, works by attaching

00:04:21.720 --> 00:04:27.780
to the adenosine receptors, and speeding up
nerve cell activity, where adenosine would

00:04:27.780 --> 00:04:33.470
slow it down. So no tea before bedtime, unless
it’s one of those herbal monstrosities.

00:04:33.470 --> 00:04:38.000
If you want to call some salad in hot water
“tea”, that’s your prerogative, I’ll

00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:44.070
be over here with my comforting beige brew.
When insomnia strikes, some people turn to

00:04:44.070 --> 00:04:49.700
alcohol or marijuana but neither is really
a great option. Both can help you to get to

00:04:49.700 --> 00:04:55.570
the early sleep stages fairly quickly but
they reduce the amount of time in stage 5,

00:04:55.570 --> 00:05:00.370
REM sleep, so you won’t wake up feeling
refreshed, and your room will smell of fosters

00:05:00.370 --> 00:05:04.770
and domino’s pizza.
When it comes to melatonin, the biggest enemy

00:05:04.770 --> 00:05:09.040
is your phone screen. It’s thought that
the biggest obstacle to most people falling

00:05:09.040 --> 00:05:16.190
asleep today is technology. The light from
a smartphone or a laptop is enough to trick

00:05:16.190 --> 00:05:22.880
your brain into thinking it’s still daylight
so you won’t produce the necessary melatonin.

00:05:22.880 --> 00:05:28.060
It can increase the time it takes you to fall
asleep by over an hour. It’s important to

00:05:28.060 --> 00:05:33.760
give yourself a good 30 minutes before bed
with no screens. You should try that new app,

00:05:33.760 --> 00:05:38.410
where people take all of the subtitles from
famous movies and print them out on sheets

00:05:38.410 --> 00:05:41.290
of paper, except more articulately written.

00:05:41.290 --> 00:05:46.650
There’s a lot of misinformation out there
about sleep, partly because there is so much

00:05:46.650 --> 00:05:51.560
we don’t understand. So let’s try to tackle
a few of the more common myths.

00:05:51.560 --> 00:05:56.360
Firstly, the obvious one; counting sheep.
It’s thought that this whole concept is

00:05:56.360 --> 00:06:01.300
derived from actual shepherds. They would
worry about their flocks going missing at

00:06:01.300 --> 00:06:05.510
night so, to make sure all was well, they
would go out and count up the little land

00:06:05.510 --> 00:06:12.100
clouds. For you and me though, with our worries
being decidedly non-ovine, counting sheep

00:06:12.100 --> 00:06:19.620
is completely unhelpful as it stimulates the
active parts of your brain. You’re much

00:06:19.620 --> 00:06:25.140
better off picturing a relaxing scene, like
a beach or a waterfall. And sure, that can

00:06:25.140 --> 00:06:30.040
include sheep if you like, just make sure
nobody else is in attendance.

00:06:30.040 --> 00:06:36.160
Exercise is important to keep you healthy
and a good active day will help you nod off.

00:06:36.160 --> 00:06:41.360
But if you’re restless, doing some sit ups
is only going to make things worse since you

00:06:41.360 --> 00:06:47.360
will increase adrenaline and, more importantly,
raise your core temperature. Heat equilibrium

00:06:47.360 --> 00:06:52.320
is important for sleep and you should be aiming
to keep your extremities warm, like with socks,

00:06:52.320 --> 00:06:57.229
but your core cool, which is easy if you have
a frosty heart.

00:06:57.229 --> 00:07:03.699
And finally, we’re often told that you can
train yourself to need less sleep. Well, you

00:07:03.699 --> 00:07:09.290
can’t. You can learn to put up with it and
maybe control your mood a bit better but the

00:07:09.290 --> 00:07:14.949
fact is, we each have a typical sleep requirement,
which differs from person to person, often

00:07:14.949 --> 00:07:20.830
7-8 hours, and with any less than that, we
won’t be running at our optimum; concentration

00:07:20.830 --> 00:07:26.660
will drop, your temper will be shorter and
you’ll find Janice in human resources that

00:07:26.660 --> 00:07:32.320
extra bit annoying. Sure, she’s irritating
on a good day but without a good sleep, she’s

00:07:32.320 --> 00:07:37.320
like listening to Paris Hilton reading 50
Shades of Grey over a severely bad Skype connection.

00:07:37.320 --> 00:07:43.740
But no matter how much sleep you manage to
get each night the best way to wake up feeling

00:07:43.740 --> 00:07:50.520
refreshed is to avoid waking up during deep-sleep.
Doing so will make you feel groggy and sleepy

00:07:50.520 --> 00:07:57.419
for hours afterwards; wake up during light-sleep
and you’ll feel awake and refreshed. But

00:07:57.419 --> 00:08:04.380
it’s not down to luck, you can actually
plan to wake up during light-sleep, every

00:08:04.380 --> 00:08:11.200
morning. It takes the brain 90 minutes to
go through one full sleep cycle. So try to

00:08:11.200 --> 00:08:17.380
always set your alarm in 90-minute intervals,
giving you the best chance of waking up during

00:08:17.380 --> 00:08:24.669
light-sleep. So you could set your alarm for
6 hours after you go to bed, or 7 and ½ hours

00:08:24.669 --> 00:08:31.680
or 9 hours, all are divisible by 90 minutes.
Just make sure you factor in the average of

00:08:31.680 --> 00:08:35.100
14 minutes it takes for a person to fall asleep.

00:08:35.100 --> 00:08:40.300
So, I hope this has helped some of the insomniacs
out there; although I’m not sure it was

00:08:40.300 --> 00:08:46.630
too smart of me to tell my entire audience
to stop staring at screens. No staring after

00:08:46.630 --> 00:08:51.200
midnight, deal? And for those of you who do
sleep like a baby, I hope you have someone

00:08:51.200 --> 00:08:54.820
to change you in the middle of the night,
because you really should have grown out of

00:08:54.820 --> 00:08:58.380
that by now. Thanks for the view, subscribe
for more Thoughty2.

